<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imphal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imphal is the capital of the state of Manipur in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, set in a fertile valley ringed by hills. It was the seat of the Meitei kings of Manipur, whose Kangla fort stands at its centre. In 1944 the Japanese offensive against Imphal, joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-army">Indian National Army</a>, was defeated in one of the decisive battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> campaign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imphal.jpg" alt="Imphal" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DeepanjanGhosh · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imphal_At_Dusk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Imphal lies in the Imphal Valley in the extreme east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, surrounded by nine ranges of hills, at an average elevation of 786 metres. Its climate is humid subtropical, with cool, dry winters and a slightly hot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> season. The name comes from two Meitei words, yum, meaning house, and phāl, meaning plenty or beautiful.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tradition holds that King Pakhangba established Kangla Fort, once the capital of Manipur, in 33 CE. The kingdom had earlier been ruled by kings of the Khaba clan; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningthouja-dynasty">Ningthouja dynasty</a> began to rule after the ascension of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nongda-lairen-pakhangba">Nongda Lairen Pakhangba</a> and came to dominate the region in politics and war. Kangla Palace was built by King Khagemba and his son Khunjaoba. In the reign of Maharaja Bhagyachandra there were a number of Burmese invasions, and the kingdom survived with the help of Maharaj Gambhir Singh.</p><p>Imphal remained peaceful until 1891, when disputes within the royal family led the British to send J. W. Quinton, and an order to arrest the prince Senapati Tikendrajit angered the people. The resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-manipur-war">Anglo-Manipur War</a> was won by the British. The king, Maharaja Kulachandra, was sentenced to imprisonment and sent to the Cellular Jail in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andaman-and-nicobar-islands">Andaman and Nicobar Islands</a>, and Tikendrajit and Thangal General were hanged. The British destroyed Kangla Palace in the war. The region merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1949.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-imphal">Battle of Imphal</a> was fought between March and July 1944. The Japanese had invaded in order to destroy the Allied forces and then invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, but were defeated and forced to retreat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Imphal is the capital of the Indian state of Manipur, with a population of 277,196 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>; most people speak Manipuri. Hinduism is the majority religion and Sanamahism the second largest, and the Pakhangba temple stands within Kangla Fort, whose ruins are surrounded by a moat. The Ima Keithel, or Mothers' Market, was established in the 16th century and is run by women vendors. The Imphal War Cemetery commemorates Indian and British soldiers who died in 1944.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Imphal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Subhas Chandra Bose (1897–1945), twice Congress president, broke with Gandhi in 1939, escaped from house arrest in 1941 and led the Indian National Army, raised with Japanese support largely from Indian prisoners of war, which fought beside the Japanese at <b>Imphal</b> and Kohima in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the following centuries the Ningthouja kings absorbed the other Meitei clans of the <b>Imphal</b> valley into a single kingdom known to its people as Kangleipak.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-manipur">Kingdom of Manipur</a></li></ul></section>
